const intro={"n":19,"title":"The Younger Son's Love for a Widow and His Forced Recruitment","nums":[829,841,853],"summary":"After leaving Vizcacha's home, the younger son avoids the judge and lives independently but in poverty. He falls in love with a widow who rejects him. Seeking relief, he consults a diviner who sells him a sequence of bizarre remedies, none of which cures his feelings. A priest eventually tells him that the widow has promised not to remarry. The son says he gives up pursuing her and later hears that the priest advised the judge to expel him from the district. Soon afterward he is seized and sent to the frontier with a contingent.","people":"Martín Fierro's younger son; a widow he wishes to marry; a paid diviner or folk healer; a priest; a judge described in a report from a friend.","themes":"Unrequited love and consent; quack remedies; credulity and exploitation; institutional power; forced frontier service; inheritance left unresolved.","words":"viuda (widow); adivino (diviner); ruda (rue plant); contingente (group drafted for service); pago or partido (home district); rodeo (herd of cattle).","questions":["What does the son hope to achieve by consulting the diviner?","Which remedies does he try, and do they work?","What reason does the priest report for the widow's refusal to marry?","What information about the judge comes from the young man's friend rather than direct observation?","How does the canto return to the unresolved property left by his aunt?"],"analysis":"The remedies are presented satirically and fail to change the widow's wishes. Her refusal must be respected rather than framed as an illness to be cured. The suggestion that a priest influenced the judge reaches the narrator through a friend, so the guide separates the report from confirmed causation. The boy's possible inheritance remains an open question.","related":"/en/students/vuelta-canto-13/","relatedTitle":"The aunt's inheritance and the judge"};
